.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt FIDOKEYCTL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fidokeyctl
.Nd inspect and manage FIDO security keys
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl cdiPpr
.Op Fl f Ar device
.Nm
.Fl D Ar credential-id
.Op Fl f Ar device
.Nm
.Fl R
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility inspects and manages FIDO authenticators using
.Xr libfido2 3 .
.Pp
It provides a small Unix-style interface for querying authenticator
capabilities, managing FIDO2 PINs, inspecting and deleting resident
credentials, and performing a guarded FIDO reset.
.Pp
If exactly one FIDO authenticator is connected, it is selected
automatically.
If multiple authenticators are present, an explicit device must be
selected with
.Fl f ,
except when using
.Fl R .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c
Display resident, or discoverable, credential information.
.Pp
The authenticator PIN is requested from the terminal.
The output includes the number of existing resident credentials,
an estimate of the remaining credential capacity, relying party
information, user information, and credential identifiers.
.Pp
The reported remaining credential count is an estimate supplied by
the authenticator and must not be interpreted as a fixed storage
capacity.
.It Fl D Ar credential-id
Delete the resident credential identified by
.Ar credential-id .
.Pp
The identifier must be specified as the hexadecimal credential
identifier displayed by
.Fl c .
.Pp
The authenticator PIN is requested from the terminal.
Before deletion,
.Nm
verifies that the credential exists, displays information about it,
and requests explicit confirmation.
.Pp
This operation is destructive and cannot be combined with other
operations.
The
.Fl f
option may be used to explicitly select the authenticator.
.It Fl d
Enable diagnostic output from
.Xr libfido2 3 .
.Pp
Debug mode cannot be combined with operations requiring PIN entry.
.It Fl f Ar device
Use the specified FIDO device, for example
.Pa /dev/fido/0 .
.Pp
If this option is omitted,
.Nm
automatically selects the authenticator when exactly one device is
available.
.It Fl i
Display information about the authenticator.
.Pp
The output includes the FIDO protocol version, PIN and user
verification capabilities, credential management support, CTAP2
versions and options, and the authenticator AAGUID.
.It Fl p
Set the initial FIDO2 PIN.
.Pp
This operation is only valid when no PIN is currently configured.
The new PIN is read from the terminal without echo and must be
entered twice for confirmation.
.It Fl P
Change an existing FIDO2 PIN.
.Pp
The current PIN and the new PIN are read from the terminal without
echo.
The new PIN must be entered twice for confirmation.
.It Fl r
Display the number of remaining FIDO2 PIN attempts.
.Pp
If no PIN is configured,
.Nm
reports:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pin-retries: not-set
.Ed
.It Fl R
Reset the FIDO application on the authenticator.
.Pp
This is a destructive operation.
It removes the configured FIDO PIN and all FIDO credentials stored
on the authenticator.
.Pp
For safety,
.Fl R
cannot be combined with any other option and exactly one FIDO
authenticator must be connected.
The user must explicitly type
.Dq RESET ,
physically remove and reinsert the authenticator, and confirm the
operation through user presence on the authenticator.
.El
.Sh PIN HANDLING
PINs are read directly from the controlling terminal using
.Xr readpassphrase 3
and are never accepted as command-line arguments.
.Pp
Sensitive PIN buffers are explicitly cleared from memory after use.
.Pp
When an incorrect PIN is supplied,
.Nm
reports the remaining number of attempts when available.
A temporary PIN authentication block may require the authenticator
to be physically removed and reinserted.
.Pp
If the PIN retry counter is exhausted, the FIDO PIN becomes blocked
and a FIDO reset may be required.
Such a reset destroys FIDO credentials stored on the authenticator.
.Sh CREDENTIAL MANAGEMENT
The
.Fl c
option uses the FIDO2 credential management interface to inspect
resident credentials stored on the authenticator.
.Pp
For each relying party,
.Nm
may display:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the relying party identifier;
.It
the relying party name;
.It
the user name;
.It
the user display name;
.It
the credential identifier.
.El
.Pp
Only resident, also known as discoverable, credentials stored on the
authenticator can be listed.
Non-resident WebAuthn credentials, such as traditional security-key
registrations, are not visible through credential management.
The absence of a credential from this output does not imply that the
authenticator has never been registered with the corresponding
service.
.Pp
The
.Fl D
option deletes one resident credential.
The credential identifier can be obtained from the output of
.Fl c .
Before deleting the credential,
.Nm
verifies the identifier against the credentials stored on the
authenticator and displays the matching relying party and user
information.
.Pp
Deleting a resident credential is permanent and may make the
corresponding passkey unusable.
.Sh FIDO RESET
The
.Fl R
operation is intentionally handled separately from other operations.
.Pp
A typical reset session is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -R
WARNING: FIDO reset will erase the PIN and all FIDO credentials
stored on this authenticator.

Type "RESET" to continue: RESET
Remove and reinsert the authenticator, then press Enter immediately.

Touch the authenticator to confirm reset.
FIDO authenticator reset
.Ed
.Pp
Authenticators may impose a limited time window after power-up during
which a reset is permitted.
The exact behaviour is authenticator-dependent.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
uses
.Xr pledge 2
to restrict available system calls and
.Xr unveil 2
to restrict filesystem visibility.
.Pp
During normal operation, filesystem access is restricted to the
selected FIDO device.
Once the authenticator has been opened, filesystem access is dropped
from the pledged capabilities.
.Pp
Operations requiring PIN input temporarily retain terminal access
until PIN entry is complete.
.Pp
During a FIDO reset,
.Nm
keeps the FIDO device namespace visible long enough to rediscover the
authenticator after it has been physically removed and reinserted.
.Pp
Debug mode is not allowed during PIN-authenticated operations in order
to avoid exposing potentially sensitive diagnostic information.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Display information about the only connected authenticator:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -i
.Ed
.Pp
Display information and the PIN retry count:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -ir
.Ed
.Pp
Select a specific authenticator:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -i -f /dev/fido/0
.Ed
.Pp
Set the initial FIDO2 PIN:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -p
New PIN:
Confirm PIN:
PIN set
.Ed
.Pp
Change an existing FIDO2 PIN:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -P
Current PIN:
New PIN:
Confirm PIN:
PIN updated
.Ed
.Pp
Display resident credential information:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -c
PIN:
resident-credentials: 2
resident-credentials-remaining: 98 (estimated)

rp-id: example.com
  credentials:
    user-name: user
    display-name: User
    credential-id: 0123456789abcdef
.Ed
.Pp
Delete a resident credential:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -D 0123456789abcdef
PIN:
rp-id: example.com
user-name: user
display-name: User
credential-id: 0123456789abcdef
Delete this resident credential? [y/N] y
resident credential deleted
.Ed
.Pp
Delete a resident credential from a specific authenticator:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -D 0123456789abcdef -f /dev/fido/1
.Ed
.Pp
Reset the FIDO authenticator:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fidokeyctl -R
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh-keygen 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr readpassphrase 3 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr fido 4 ,
.Xr fido_cbor_info_new 3 ,
.Xr fido_credman_metadata_new 3 ,
.Xr fido_dev_info_manifest 3 ,
.Xr fido_dev_open 3 ,
.Xr fido_dev_set_pin 3
.Sh AUTHORS
